k quickly.\n\n#### 1. The Problem with Linear Search\nIf you had to look for a book by checking every shelf one by one, it would take a long time. This is like searching through a list.\n\n#### 2. The Hash Map Solution\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to assign each book a unique **address** (or \"bucket\") in the library.\n\n- **Key**: The title of the book (e.g., \"Harry Potter\").\n- **Hash Function**: A mathematical formula that turns the title into a number (e.g., 42).\n- **Bucket**: The shelf number where the book is stored (Shelf 42).\n\n**How it works:**\n1. You ask for \"Harry Potter\".\n2. The hash function calculates the number 42.\n3. You go directly to Shelf 42.\n4. You find the book immediately.\n\nThis is **O(1)** time complexity — meaning it takes roughly the same amount of time regardless of how many books are in the library.\n\n---", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.6890849000046728, "decode_s": 4.830420500002219, "total_s": 5.520293700006732, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 52.790435118409015, "pp": 43.53599970017709}}
