 two primary transport protocols used in the Internet. They differ fundamentally in how they handle data delivery, reliability, and speed.\n\nHere is a detailed comparison based on your requested criteria:\n\n### 1. Guarantees\n\n| Feature | TCP | UDP |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Reliability** | **Guaranteed.** TCP ensures that data is delivered correctly and in order. It uses acknowledgments (ACKs) and retransmissions. | **No guarantee.** UDP sends packets without checking if they arrive. It is \"fire and forget.\" |\n| **Ordering** | **Guaranteed.** Packets are delivered in the exact order they were sent. | **No guarantee.** Packets may arrive out of order or not at all. |\n| **Connection** | **Connection-oriented.** A handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) is required before data transfer begins. | **Connectionless.** No handshake; data is sent immediately. |\n| **Flow Control** | **Yes.** TCP uses sliding windows to prevent overwhelming the receiver. | **No.** UDP does not manage flow control. |\n\n### ", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.6899536000055377, "decode_s": 4.820426399994176, "total_s": 5.5108106000043335, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 52.899884541398265, "pp": 43.48118482135496}}
